    "prose": "Duru set off from the harbor and moved onto the coastal path. Ekin waited one stubborn heartbeat, then moved onto the coastal path after her.\n\nWind pressed at Ekin’s jacket as the harbor fell behind them. The path bent along the rising headland, and Duru kept ahead, guiding them along each turn without hesitation.\n\n“Stay on this side,” Duru called over the wind. “The coastal route goes straight to the open lighthouse door.”\n\n“I can see the path,” Ekin replied.\n\nAt the next bend, however, she started toward the other side. Duru stopped and pointed along the continuing coastal route. The lighthouse stood ahead, its open upper-room door facing the path.\n\nEkin looked from Duru’s choice to the doorway. Duru’s route was the right one, and Ekin knew it.\n\n“Fine,” she said. “Lead the way. I’m following.”\n\nThey hurried on as thunder rolled nearer. Ekin kept searching the sky, but there was no sign of the sea dragon or the stolen compass. Every empty moment made her want to run faster.\n\nDuru reached the lighthouse first. She moved through the open upper-room door and entered the lighthouse upper room.\n\nEkin reached the same doorway. She moved through it and entered the lighthouse upper room after Duru.\n\nNow both girls stood inside the upper room. Ekin remained close to the entrance, guarded and impatient.\n\n“You got me here,” she told Duru. “That doesn’t mean I trust you.”\n\nDuru nodded. “Then don’t. Just keep going with me.”\n\nEkin glanced into the room. “Until I get my compass back.”\n\n“Until then,” Duru agreed.",
